  :adr/body "# ADR 2799008131: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8131 Chemical Products Plant and Machine Operators chemical-plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8131\n-- Chemical Products Plant and Machine Operators** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout: Wave0/1/2 100% complete, the\nentire 75xx craft-trades range is now complete, and this batch adds\nfurther coverage in the 81xx/82xx plant-and-machine-operator range\n(several sibling 81xx occupations -- e.g. 8121, 8153, 8154, 8160,\n8172, 8189 -- were already `:implemented` prior to this ADR, so this\nis not the first entry into that range, but this ADR's specific unit\ngroup, 8131, had no prior repository).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8131\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (raw grep of\nthe registry's pr-str'd `:kotoba.occupation/occupations` blob, since a\nlocal `clojure -M -e` invocation of `kotoba.occupation/get-occupation`\nrequires the `kotoba-lang/technology` sibling checkout at a relative\npath this session's isolated scratch clone did not have available;\nthe blob-level grep is an equally direct read of the same source-of-\ntruth EDN and is the technique this task's own CRITICAL section\ndescribes for counting/verifying registry entries), and its `:name`\nread exactly \"Chemical Products Plant and Machine Operators\" --\nconfirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and\n`:operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and\nno `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at `cloud-itonami/\ncloud-itonami-isco-8131` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before scaffolding).\nThis ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `8131` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\n**Reference-repo correction.** The task's cited reference,\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8111` (Miners and Quarriers), does\nNOT exist -- confirmed 404 via both `git clone` and `gh api` -- and the\nregistry itself still lists `8111` as `:maturity :spec`. This is a\nstale/incorrect reference in the task brief, not a landed repo; no\nwork was attempted against it. This session used the task's other\ncited comparable-hazard reference instead,\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7542`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7542)\n(Shotfirers and Blasters: confirmed live, `:implemented`, pushed\n2026-07-18), cloned and read in full this session -- its `store`/\n`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split, full doc set, and closed-allowlist/\nscope-exclusion pattern for a comparable explosives-handling\nlife-safety-critical hazard were mirrored closely, adapting the\nexclusion language to chemical-process terms. `cloud-itonami/\ncloud-itonami-isco-8121` (Metal Processing Plant Operators, same 81xx\nplant-operator subgroup, already `:implemented`) was also cloned and\nread for comparison, but it predates the `langgraph.graph`\nactor/advisor/governor/store StateGraph pattern (it has only a bare\n`governor`+`store`, no `actor.cljc`/`advisor.cljc`, no langgraph\ndependency) and its registry entry uses a different\n`:required-technologies` set (`:telemetry`/`:dmn`/`:bpmn` instead of\n`:identity`) than 8131's own pre-existing `:spec` entry -- so 8121 was\nnot used as the structural template; 7542's shape, which matches\n8131's pre-existing `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]` exactly, was used instead.\n\n**This actor carries a chemical-plant life-safety-critical hazard\nframing, comparable to the mining (ISCO 8111, registry `:spec`) and\nblasting (ISCO 7542, `:implemented`) hazard framings already\nestablished in this catalog.** Chemical Products Plant and Machine\nOperators run industrial chemical processing plants -- real risk of\ntoxic release, fire, explosion or reaction hazards if process controls\nfail, categorically higher-stakes than ordinary workshop trades. The\nclosed op allowlist therefore must NEVER include any op that directly\nfinalizes a chemical-process-execution decision (authorizing a\nprocess/reaction to proceed) or a plant-safety-clearance decision, nor\noverride a plant safety officer's judgment -- these are always a hard,\npermanent, structural block (absent from the allowlist entirely, not\nmerely gated), never auto-commit-eligible under any confidence level.\nAny safety/anomaly flag (process anomaly, leak risk, equipment\ncondition) ALWAYS escalates, no exceptions, ever.\n\n**This actor coordinates CHEMICAL-PLANT SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY --\nit never operates chemical-processing equipment itself and never\nmakes a chemical-process-execution or plant-safety-clearance\ndecision.** It is not an authority to decide when or whether a process\nruns; it is a coordination layer for production-run/batch/progress\nrecord logging, crew/shift scheduling, safety-concern surfacing and\nadministrative/equipment supply order coordination (never process\nchemicals or reagents themselves -- chemical handling is entirely out\nof scope for this administrative-coordination actor), all gated\nbehind an independent governor. It never finalizes a chemical-process-\nexecution decision and never performs or completes a chemical process.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8131` as an OSS\ndocumentation/coordination actor for chemical products plant and\nmachine operators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as\nevery prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Chemical-\nPlant Scheduling & Logistics Coordination Advisor ⊣ ChemCoordGovernor**\non a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- verified\nagainst three other freshly-landed sibling repos in this same batch,\nall using the same pin -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger. All source is\nportable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests are `clojure.test` `.clj`,\nmatching the reference repo's own layout.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/batch/progress data logging\n  (administrative metadata only)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a process-anomaly/leak-risk/\n  equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible, no confidence-level exception, ever\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- administrative/equipment procurement\n  proposal (NOT process chemicals or reagents themselves -- chemical\n  handling is entirely out of scope for this administrative-\n  coordination actor), escalates above a registered cost threshold\n\nNo op in this allowlist finalizes a chemical-process-execution\ndecision (authorizing a process/reaction to proceed), finalizes a\nplant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant safety officer's\njudgment -- these decision classes are structurally absent from the\nallowlist, not merely gated behind escalation.\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (`chemcoord.governor`, permanent, never\noverridable by human approval)\n\n1. **no-plant** -- the operator/plant record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action.\n2. **no-actuation** -- proposal `:effect` must be `:propose` (the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never operates chemical-\n   processing equipment).\n3. **unknown-op** -- `:op` must be one of the four allowlisted\n   coordination ops; the closed op-allowlist is enforced\n   unconditionally.\n4. **plant-mismatch** -- if the proposal names a plant, it must be the\n   SAME plant verified for this request (defense-in-depth against a\n   proposal quietly targeting an unverified plant).\n5. **unknown-operator / operator-wrong-plant** -- if the proposal\n   references an operator, that operator must be a REGISTERED\n   certified chemical-plant operator belonging to this plant.\n6. **scope-exclusion-violation** -- a proposal that attempts, in any\n   free-text field, to authorize a process/reaction to proceed, to\n   finalize a plant-safety-clearance decision, or to override a plant\n   safety officer's judgment is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content, independent of `:op`.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (`chemcoord.governor/always-escalate-ops`). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a process-anomaly/leak-\n  risk/equipment-condition concern -- it never resolves the concern\n  itself, and this is unconditional: no confidence level, however\n  high, ever exempts a safety flag from human sign-off.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (20000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` (0.6) also escalates,\n  for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare nouns (e.g.\n\"chemical\", \"reaction\", \"process\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this risk is especially\nsharp here because this actor's ENTIRE domain is chemical-plant\ncoordination, so bare nouns like \"chemical\"/\"reaction\"/\"process\"/\n\"batch\"/\"reagent\" are ordinary, unavoidable vocabulary.\n`chemcoord.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g. \"authorize the process to\nproceed\", \"finalize the chemical-process operation\", \"override the\nplant safety officer's judgment\"), never bare nouns, and\n`chemcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor (including a\n`:flag-safety-concern` request whose description names an \"unresolved\nleak-risk concern near unit 3 batch 12, equipment-condition review\npending\"), never trips the scope-exclusion guard. A companion test,\n`scope-exclusion-not-triggered-by-bare-domain-nouns`, asserts directly\nagainst `governor/scope-exclusion-violation?` that a rationale/\ndescription containing only bare domain nouns (\"proposed schedule-\ncrew-operation for batch 12 chemical-process preparation on plant\nCP-1\", \"operator fully certified for reagent handling and reaction\nprocedure documentation for this batch\") does not trip the guard,\nwhile `hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-authorize-process-to-proceed`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-chemical-process-operation`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-plant-safety-clearance`\nand\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-plant-safety-officer`\nconfirm the actual forbidden action phrases DO trip it, and\n`holds-a-scope-excluded-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`,\n`holds-a-finalize-chemical-process-operation-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nand\n`holds-an-override-plant-safety-officer-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nconfirm the full actor graph resolves such attempts straight to\n`:hold` (a finish point, never `:request-approval`) with no record\ncommitted.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8131\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8131` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8131`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry, not\n  assumed, immediately before the edit to account for concurrent\n  sibling landings in this same batch -- six sibling agents were\n  landing occupations concurrently).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 26 tests, 66 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository (mojibake scan: 0\n  replacement characters found).\n- `clojure -M:lint` (clj-kondo): 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-7542`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n- No op that finalizes a chemical-process-execution decision, finalizes\n  a plant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant safety\n  officer's judgment exists anywhere in this actor's code -- this is a\n  structural absence (closed allowlist), not a runtime-gated\n  exclusion, and is the load-bearing safety property this ADR records.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-process-authorization` or\n  `:authorize-process` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops`: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  chemical-plant-safety guidance -- an always-escalate op still means\n  the *capability* to finalize a chemical-process-execution decision\n  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For\n  a decision that can directly cause toxic release, fire, explosion or\n  reaction hazards from a chemical-process-control failure, the\n  capability itself must not exist in the allowlist at all -- this\n  matches the strict bar already established by\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7542` (blast-authorization decisions) and the\n  registry's mining framing (ISCO 8111).\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"chemical\" \"reaction\"\n  \"process\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip bug class --\n  this actor's entire domain is chemical-plant coordination, so bare\n  nouns would self-trip on essentially every legitimate proposal\n  (work-record logs naming \"batch 12 progress notes\", crew-schedule\n  proposals naming \"shift changeover\", safety flags naming \"leak-risk\n  concern\", supply orders naming \"plant PPE and administrative unit-3\n  procurement signage\", etc.). Scope-exclusion terms were phrased\n  exclusively as multi-word finalization/execution ACTION phrases\n  instead, per the task's explicit self-tripping-bug-pattern guidance,\n  and a dedicated regression test asserts the default mock advisor's\n  proposals across every allowlisted op never self-trip.\n- Checking plant/operator provenance only from the proposal (never the\n  request): rejected -- the plant-mismatch check requires the plant\n  identity to be anchored at the request level and independently\n  cross-checked against anything the proposal claims, so a proposal\n  that quietly names a different, unverified plant is caught even if\n  the proposal otherwise looks well-formed.\n- Modeling the operator-registration check as unconditionally required\n  on every op: rejected as too rigid -- `:coordinate-supply-order`\n  legitimately has no operator to reference (it is a materials-\n  procurement proposal against the plant, not a person); the check\n  applies only when a proposal actually references an `:operator-id`.\n- Allowing `:coordinate-supply-order` to cover process chemicals or\n  reagents: rejected outright per the task's explicit scope guidance\n  -- this actor coordinates CHEMICAL-PLANT SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY,\n  and process-chemical/reagent handling is entirely out of scope; the\n  op's docstring and every fixture in this repo name only plant-\n  equipment/administrative-supply items (plant PPE, procurement\n  signage), never process chemicals or reagents themselves.\n- Using `cloud-itonami-isco-8121` (Metal Processing Plant Operators,\n  same 81xx plant-operator subgroup) as the structural template\n  instead of 7542: rejected -- 8121 predates the `langgraph.graph`\n  actor/advisor/governor/store StateGraph pattern used by every other\n  actor in this fleet (it has only a bare `governor`+`store`) and its\n  registry `:required-technologies` differ from 8131's own\n  pre-existing `:spec` entry; 7542's shape and required-technologies\n  matched exactly, and its explosives-handling hazard framing is the\n  closest comparable life-safety-critical pattern already landed, so\n  it was used as the template instead, per the task's explicit\n  fallback guidance to the closest comparable life-safety-critical\n  pattern.\n"}]
